WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.000
PlantUML Ultimate turns a plain text plan into a chart you
can edit visually.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.920
The source remains readable and suitable for Git review.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:16.915
This short tour uses a few prepared examples to show task
editing, people, workload and version history.

00:00:17.035 --> 00:00:20.735
Each example focuses on one planning decision.

00:00:20.855 --> 00:00:24.230
Keep the text and the chart together as you work.

00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:30.433
Add a task from the Insert tab.

00:00:30.553 --> 00:00:34.603
Give it a meaningful name and a duration in working days.

00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:39.223
Choose a start date rather than relying on an accidental
default.

00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:42.118
Review the fields before adding it.

00:00:42.238 --> 00:00:45.738
The new task appears in the chart and in the source.

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.242
Click a task to open its inspector.

00:00:52.362 --> 00:00:56.262
Edit the duration, then leave the field to commit the
change.

00:00:56.382 --> 00:00:59.582
The source records the new effort estimate.

00:00:59.702 --> 00:01:04.252
Check the chart after the edit, including tasks that
depend on it.

00:01:04.372 --> 00:01:07.772
Close the inspector when you are ready to continue.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.725
Progress is another explicit task property.

00:01:14.845 --> 00:01:17.345
Open the task inspector.

00:01:17.465 --> 00:01:20.665
Enter twenty five percent in Progress.

00:01:20.785 --> 00:01:24.735
The matching completion statement is written into the
source.

00:01:24.855 --> 00:01:28.680
Keep this observation current when reporting the project.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.825
Close the inspector to review the plan.

00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:38.408
People are assigned in the task inspector.

00:01:38.528 --> 00:01:42.378
Open Resources to see a person and their allocation.

00:01:42.498 --> 00:01:45.648
An allocation is a scheduling assumption.

00:01:45.768 --> 00:01:48.943
Review it alongside the effort estimate.

00:01:49.063 --> 00:01:55.188
A larger percentage needs a practical explanation, not
just a desire for an earlier finish.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.125
Here Backend has eight days of effort and one part time
assignment.

00:02:03.245 --> 00:02:06.820
Open Resources to inspect that starting point.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.615
Add Alex at fifty percent as a second person.

00:02:10.735 --> 00:02:15.510
Both assignments are retained while the effort estimate
stays at eight days.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:22.330
The working duration is calculated from the combined
allocation; check the task tooltip to see the result.

00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:28.358
Open Plan, then Workload.

00:02:28.478 --> 00:02:32.928
This example deliberately assigns Alice to overlapping
tasks.

00:02:33.048 --> 00:02:38.023
The peak reaches two hundred percent against her one
hundred percent capacity.

00:02:38.143 --> 00:02:41.318
Read the warning as a conflict to resolve.

00:02:41.438 --> 00:02:46.713
Click Frontend to return to the responsible task and
inspect its assignment.

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:50.433
Workload links the warning to the plan you can change.

00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:58.208
Capacity changes the assumption used to evaluate workload.

00:02:58.328 --> 00:03:00.803
Open Plan and Workload.

00:03:00.923 --> 00:03:05.798
Enter a different capacity only when it represents real
availability.

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:08.843
Commit the field and compare the warning.

00:03:08.963 --> 00:03:15.763
A capacity of two hundred percent may describe a team
resource; it does not create more hours for one person.

00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:19.533
Keep that distinction visible when you explain a plan.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:26.233
Version history makes changes reviewable.

00:03:26.353 --> 00:03:31.278
In this example we create a checkpoint, then change the
Design estimate.

00:03:31.398 --> 00:03:36.248
Open File and Version history, and select the checkpoint
for comparison.

00:03:36.368 --> 00:03:39.918
Choose Source to inspect the exact changed lines.

00:03:40.038 --> 00:03:44.588
This gives a concrete record to discuss before accepting a
new schedule.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.150
The same comparison has a Rendered view.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:55.270
Select it to see the earlier chart beside the working
copy.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:59.215
Review the scheduling consequence as well as the text
diff.

00:03:59.335 --> 00:04:05.435
Tasks, people, workload and history all remain tied to a
readable PlantUML document.

00:04:05.555 --> 00:04:10.505
Commit the text to Git and reopen it here whenever visual
editing is useful.
